      "id": "q-1783255802814-e590f",
      "order": 7,
      "question": "A cyclist covers a certain distance at a speed of 15 km/h. If he had travelled 5 km/h faster, he would have taken 1 hour less to cover the same distance. What is the distance?",
      "type": "mcq",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "45 km",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "index": 0,
          "imageUrl": "",
          "explanation": "Incorrect calculation."
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "50 km",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "index": 1,
          "imageUrl": "",
          "explanation": "Incorrect calculation."
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "60 km",
          "isCorrect": true,
          "index": 2,
          "imageUrl": "",
          "explanation": "D = 15 * T = 15 * 4 = 60 km."
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "75 km",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "index": 3,
          "imageUrl": "",
          "explanation": "Incorrect calculation."
        }
      ],
      "answer": "",
      "alternateAnswers": [],
      "explanation": "Let the distance be D km and the original time taken be T hours. Original speed (S1) = 15 km/h. Equation 1: D = 15 * T. New speed (S2) = 15 + 5 = 20 km/h. New time = T - 1 hour. Equation 2: D = 20 * (T - 1). Since the distance D is the same: 15 * T = 20 * (T - 1). 15T = 20T - 20. 20 = 20T - 15T. 20 = 5T. T = 20 / 5 = 4 hours. Now, substitute T back into Equation 1 to find D: D = 15 * T = 15 * 4 = 60 km.",
      "hint": "",
      "marks": 5,
      "negativeMark": 1,
      "negativeMarking": true,
      "imageUrl": "",
      "subject": "cuet-gat",
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "hasError": false
    },
